## TODO: mod-operation-failure-contract

Finish remaining failure propagation gaps; do not repeat #67's state-load/save,
activation rollback, companion-move or renderer bulk-action work. Recheck these
d71d343 findings against the checkout before implementation. Rust paths below are
relative to src-tauri/src/commands/:

- mods/install.rs::uninstall_mod_op logs removal failures, then removes the saved
  record and can return success, including for host packs. Preserve tracking when
  removal fails and report partial deletion honestly. The previous implementation
  deliberately used best-effort uninstall, so this changes that behavior, not just
  a function signature. Do not describe deletion as reversible.
- mods/crimeboss_settings.rs::sync_enabled discards set_enabled_in_file's Result.
  Propagate real read/parse/write errors through activation and rollback. Preserve
  legitimate no-ops: a fresh mod may have no game-created settings file, and an
  unverified launcher path must not be guessed. Missing optional state differs from
  failure to update existing state.

Contract: success means the requested change happened. Failure preserves tracking or
explicitly reports what changed and what remains. Use existing operation functions and
rollback helpers; no generic transaction framework or broad engine rewrite.
Verify failed uninstall with files still present, partial companion deletion, Crime
Boss settings failures and failed rollback. Check command results, UI feedback/refresh
and success analytics together. Preserve #67's regression tests. New product strings
belong only in en.json.

