## TODO: i18n-english-only-enforcement

Resolve the mismatch between CLAUDE.md's translation ownership policy and CI. Product
work may edit en.json only and must not require synchronizing other locales. At d71d343,
an in-memory checkI18nSnapshot passed for the current tree but failed after adding one
English key alone, demanding scaffold-added operations in all five target locales.
Inspect scripts/i18n-enforcement.mjs, i18n-sync.mjs, i18n-history.mjs, the staged hook
and repository-root .github/workflows/ci.yml.

NON-NEGOTIABLE: Git remains authoritative evidence for acceptance and the three states:
Accepted, Missing, Review (pending in code). When English changes since acceptance,
an existing translation needs Review; its presence is not approval of the new meaning.
Missing translations remain valid and fall back to English. The owner explicitly
rejected replacing history with hashes, a metadata file, coverage percentages or
current-tree-only heuristics. Never auto-accept review debt.

Trace derived historical state versus stored ! / ? markers first. English-only changes
should pass while consumers recognize Missing and Review without mandatory target-file
writes. Check runtime behavior too: permitting unmaterialized Review in CI must not
silently change pending-value or incompatible-placeholder fallback rules. Retain explicit
translator fill/sync/review operations, structural validation and honest reporting when
history is unavailable. If marker materialization prevents this, document the exact
dependency before proposing a semantic change; preserving three-state truth comes first.

Verify with fixtures: English additions, changed English with accepted translations,
explicit Keep/Edit, source reversion, committed/staged/working-tree snapshots and
read-only commands that mutate no files. Preserve existing historical acceptance and
provenance semantics. Do not edit actual non-English locales during this task.

