## TODO: manifest-backfill-and-size

Phase 3, after pak-entries-schema ships. Trigger the manifest backfill, then measure the
real per-game snapshot size increase, then decide. The decision was deliberately deferred
until the number is real, and that still holds.

REWRITTEN 2026-08-01: the old estimate (+8-12 MB on a ~17.6 MB index.db, "paid by every
app user on the 1-hour TTL download") described the retired single-file delivery. The cost
model is now materially better and the old numbers must not be reused:

- Snapshots are per game, so only PD3 and Crime Boss users pay anything at all. PD2, PDTH
  and RAID snapshots gain nothing and grow by nothing.
- mod_index.rs caches each snapshot by sha256 and re-downloads only when the catalog
  manifest advertises a new hash, so the cost is per publish, not hourly per user.
- Because export-sqlite.ts decides what enters a snapshot, "ship manifests for one game
  only" is now a real option that the single-DB design could not express.

Measure the delta per game, not in aggregate. Options if it is too heavy, in order of
preference:

1. Keep as is (if the real number is modest)
2. Dedupe entry paths into a separate paths table with a junction (popular assets are
   overridden by many mods, so paths repeat heavily and this compresses well)
3. Publish manifests as a separate per-game R2 object the app fetches lazily, only when a
   conflict check actually needs it. This is the old "third release asset" idea, restated
   for R2, and it fits the catalog manifest cleanly since that already lists per-game
   objects with hashes.

