## TODO: nexus-update-detection

Replace the per-mod Nexus update trickle with one batched check per game, and make
per-file supersession exact. VERIFIED 2026-08-07; supersedes the quota-shaped design
notes that used to live in nexus-feature-parity.

User problem: Nexus update checks are slow (one request per installed mod, 2s stagger)
and version-string-only, so a mod whose author replaced a file without bumping the
mod-level version never surfaces, and large libraries burn quota.

Verified API surface:

- BATCH (live-verified): modsByUid(uids: [gameId*2^32+modId]) returns name, version,
  updatedAt for many mods in one request. This is the ModWorkshop ids[] equivalent the
  old notes said did not exist. modFilesByUid batches file lists the same way.
- Per-file supersession (doc-verified via the v1 API docs and Nexus's own
  node-nexus-api types; live-confirm with a signed-in session): v1 files.json returns
  file_updates chains (old_file_id -> new_file_id with timestamps), and
  /games/{domain}/mods/updated.json?period=1d|1w|1m returns { mod_id,
  latest_file_update, latest_mod_activity } per changed mod.
- v2 ModFile (live-verified) carries fileId, name, version, category, sizeInBytes
  (string BigInt - reuse parse_big_int), date, changelogText - but NO successor links,
  so exact supersession stays a v1 files.json job.

Proposed algorithm (per game, background, behind waitForForegroundClear):

1. One modsByUid batch over installed Nexus mods' uids -> updatedAt + version per mod.
2. Mods whose updatedAt is not newer than the locally recorded check time are done.
3. For the changed few, fetch v1 files.json once each and walk file_updates from the
   stored file_remote_id to the chain head; a successor existing = exact update, and
   the successor's file_id/name feed the update UI. Chains can have several hops -
   walk to the end, guard against cycles.
4. No file_updates entry for the installed file: fall back to the current
   version-string compare, never claim an exact update. latest_mod_activity alone
   (description edits) must not trigger an update prompt - that is the main
   false-positive trap. Files with category ARCHIVED/OLD_VERSION and optional files
   with no successor are the main false-negative trap: absence of a chain entry is
   "unknown", not "current".

Required stored identifiers already exist: InstalledMod.remote_id, file_remote_id, and
the source registry's numeric game ids. Modules: nexus.rs (batch query + files.json
call), nexusModCache.ts (replace the stagger loop), useModData's Nexus branch,
mods/types.rs only if a last-checked timestamp is added. Tests: uid math round-trip,
chain walk (multi-hop, cycle guard, no-successor), updatedAt short-circuit, and a
fixture from a real files.json. Coordinate with enabling version in SEARCH_QUERY
(nexus-feature-parity item 2) so update prompts come from this check, not from stale
search snapshots.

Policy: batched lookups of the user's own installed mods are ordinary API use; the v2
docs warn the schema can change without notice, so keep the v1 version-compare path as
the fallback rather than deleting it. Unresolved: whether Mod.updatedAt moves on
non-file edits (measure during implementation); viewerUpdateAvailable semantics
(auth-scoped, untested).

