## TODO: nexus-policy-boundaries

Permanent reference plus the questions only Nexus can answer. Not a task; do not
"complete" this entry. Language below distinguishes what Nexus states outright from
what is inference - do not upgrade inference to policy when citing this.

STATED by Nexus (AUP, read 2026-07-29, re-checked 2026-08-06; API docs; first-party
client docs):

- No fetching data en masse to rehost ("scraping"), no mass downloading, no serving
  Nexus data from Modrex infrastructure. This is why there is no Nexus content index
  and why gallery/requirements scraping is off the table even where the API lacks the
  data.
- Free-tier download links require the website-generated key/expires; the website
  click is the authorization. No workaround is acceptable in any form.
- Public applications must be registered (Modrex is) and send Application-Name/
  -Version on every request (nexus_headers does).
- Do not store users' tokens server-side. Modrex stores them locally only.
- The v2 GraphQL API is explicitly "in development" and may change without warning -
  every v2-dependent feature keeps a fallback or degrades gracefully.

INFERENCE (researched 2026-07-29, unchanged): a crowd-sourced hash-to-identity map
built from per-user lookups is effectively closed under the AUP plus the ToS database
-rights position, even though each individual lookup is legitimate. Treat as requiring
written Nexus permission, i.e. do not build absent that.

QUESTIONS for Nexus support (owner sends manually; registered-app channel):

1. Batch metadata: modsByUid/modFilesByUid now exist in v2 - are they intended for
   third-party managers at our scale, and is there a stability/deprecation channel
   registered apps should watch?
2. Mod galleries: not exposed on Mod and media cannot filter by mod - is exposing a
   mod's image gallery to registered apps planned?
3. Per-inner-file hashes on ModFileContent (standing community request) - any plans?
   This would replace our name/size heuristics with exact matching.
4. Adult content: do authenticated API responses respect the account's content
   preferences automatically, or are clients expected to filter on adultContent
   themselves (what we will do by default either way)?
5. Refresh tokens: do they rotate on use? (Determines whether our serialized refresh
   can ever relax.)
6. modRequirements: shape is live but our samples returned empty - is population
   complete for recent games, or partial?

